/**
\mainpage Yoyo Plugin development

Welcome to the Yoyo guide for plugin development!

Yoyo offers a plugin interface to allow the creation of new types of display- or Gui elements, new
types of data serialization objects and new types of communication.

\section main_concepts Concepts
\subsection main_concepts_naming Nomenclature
Yoyo uses a certain naming conventions, which are going to be clarified in this section.

The basic data structure for Yoyo's data model is the node \see \ref main_concepts_structure.

The nodes are classified according to the realm of functionality they belong to. The following
realms are defined:
- Data: data nodes (or signals as they are called in the Yoyo display, but we stick here to data to
  avoid conflicts with the used Qt signal and slot mechanism) can be viewed as similar to variables.
  They hold and propagate values to connected nodes. Data nodes can be connected to any other node
  type, but not to data nodes themselves.
  Even though it is supported to provide new types of data, it is most probably not necesary since
  Yoyo provides a variety of value types already.
- Gui: gui nodes represent an item in the central display. The gui nodes Yoyo already provides
  include buttons, check-boxes and line-edits. These are simple items, but a gui node can become
  more complex as well, not only providing title and unit labels as the line-edits do.
- Communication: communication nodes provide an interface to external data sources. One example
  would be a TCP-connection or CAN-connection. Communication nodes only offer the communication
  itself, sending and receiving data. They do not serialize or deserialize data themselves, but
  write the raw data to a connected data node.
- Protocol: protocol nodes are responsible for encoding and decoding (serialize/deserialize) data
  provided from/to a communication node.

\subsection main_concepts_structure Structure
In Yoyo all these objects are organized in a tree structure, which you will be familiar with. The
items making up the tree are called "nodes" and each node corresponds to some functionality. This
functionality can be grouping of other objects, so the grouping nodes will be inner nodes. The leaf
nodes, which have no children or cannot have children, carry in many cases most of the
functionality, but this doesn't always need to be the case.

When you start implementing new functionality for Yoyo, you will be implementing new nodes. For
these nodes, you will need to decide:
- is it a leaf node or an inner node: does it accept children or not
- for an inner node:
    - which kind of children will it accept
    - are there things to be done, when children are added or removed
- are there restrictions to the kind of parents, the node accepts

For each of these questions, there is a purely virtual method to be implemented in the class you
will be deriving from (or it's base class).

\note You don't need to implement parent-child management. This is done by the base class of all
nodes: \a node_base.
\see Please consult the page about \ref nodes for more information.

\subsection main_concepts_properties Properties
Any data of a node, which needs to be configured or persisted, needs to be represented by a
property. Yoyo depends heavily on the Qt-libraries and in praticular the Qt property system; all
configurable data must be represented by a Qt-property.

Please consult the page about \ref properties for more information.

\subsection main_concepts_factories Factories
Yoyo uses factories for managing the creation of nodes. There are several factories used throughout
Yoyo specializing in a certain type of nodes each. As a plugin developer you will find factories
for data nodes, gui nodes, protocol nodes and communication nodes.
*/
